{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbbUDGexYQLF1PyMQstV", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "A list is mutable and a tuple is immutable. That's the core difference.\n\n| | **List** | **Tuple** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Syntax | `[1, 2, 3]` | `(1, 2, 3)` |\n| Mutable | Yes: you can add, remove, or change items | No: contents are fixed after creation |\n| Hashable | No, so it can't be a dict key or set member | Yes, if all its items are hashable |\n| Methods | Many (`append`, `remove`, `sort`, and others) | Only `count` and `index` |\n| Memory and speed | Slightly more memory, slightly slower | Smaller and slightly faster |\n\n```python\nnums = [1, 2, 3]\nnums[0] = 99        # fine\nnums.append(4)      # fine\n\npoint = (1, 2)\npoint[0] = 99       # TypeError: 'tuple' object does not support item assignment\n```\n\n**When to use which**\n- **List:** a collection that changes or grows, usually of similar items, such as a list of users or a queue of tasks.\n- **Tuple:** a fixed record whose positions have meaning, such as `(x, y)` coordinates or `(name, age)`. Tuples are also used for dict keys, for returning multiple values from a function, and as constants.\n\n**Caveats**\n- A one-element tuple needs a trailing comma: `(1,)`. `(1)` is just the integer `1`.\n- Tuples are only shallowly immutable. A tuple holding a list can't be re-pointed to a different list, but the list inside can still be mutated: `t = ([1], 2); t[0].append(5)` works."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7293, "cache_read_input_tokens": 11903, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7293}, "output_tokens": 3, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": 